# * # g* g#, wrapscan, and the last search pattern shared with :s.

=== * searches for the whole keyword under the cursor
ref:    pattern.txt *star*
quote:  Only whole keywords are searched for, like with the command "/\<keyword\>".
given:  f|oo foobar foo
keys:   *
expect: foo foobar |foo

=== * uses the first keyword after the cursor
ref:    pattern.txt *star*
quote:  2. the first keyword after the cursor, in the current line
given:  |  bar x bar
keys:   *
expect:
    bar x |bar

=== * on punctuation uses the non-blank word under the cursor
ref:    pattern.txt *star*
quote:  3. the non-blank word under the cursor
given:  |+++ --- +++
keys:   *
expect: +++ --- |+++

=== * with a count
ref:    pattern.txt *star*
quote:  Search forward for the [count]'th occurrence of the word nearest to the cursor.
given:  |ab ab ab ab
keys:   2*
expect: ab ab |ab ab

=== * is not affected by 'ignorecase' being off
ref:    pattern.txt *star*
quote:  'ignorecase' is used, 'smartcase' is not.
given:  |ab AB ab
keys:   *
expect: ab AB |ab

=== # searches backward for the whole keyword
ref:    pattern.txt *#*
quote:  Same as "*", but search backward.
given:  foo foobar f|oo
keys:   #
expect: |foo foobar foo

=== g* also finds the word inside other words
ref:    pattern.txt *gstar*
quote:  This makes the search also find matches that are not a whole word.
given:  f|oo foobar foo
keys:   g*
expect: foo |foobar foo

=== g# also finds the word inside other words
ref:    pattern.txt *g#*
quote:  This makes the search also find matches that are not a whole word.
given:  barfoo x f|oo
keys:   g#
expect: bar|foo x foo

=== n after * keeps searching for the word
ref:    pattern.txt *star*
quote:  Only whole keywords are searched for, like with the command "/\<keyword\>".
given:  |ab abc ab ab
keys:   *n
expect: ab abc ab |ab

=== n after # keeps searching backward
ref:    pattern.txt *#*
quote:  Same as "*", but search backward.
given:  ab ab ab a|b
keys:   #n
expect: ab |ab ab ab

=== :s is a jump
ref:    motion.txt *jump-motions*
quote:  following commands are "jump" commands: "'", "`", "G", "/", "?", "n", "N", "%", "(", ")", "[[", "]]", "{", "}", ":s"
given:
  |ab
  ab
keys:   :2s/a/x/<CR>
expect:
  ab
  |xb
jumps:  1:0

=== * on the only occurrence wraps back to it
ref:    options.txt *'wrapscan'*
quote:  Searches wrap around the end of the file.
given:  x |ab y
keys:   *
expect: x |ab y

=== / wraps around the end of the buffer
ref:    pattern.txt *last-pattern*
quote:  If the 'wrapscan' option is on (which is the default), searches wrap around the end of the buffer.
given:
  foo a
  b|ar
keys:   /foo<CR>
expect:
  |foo a
  bar

=== ? wraps around the start of the buffer
ref:    pattern.txt *last-pattern*
quote:  If the 'wrapscan' option is on (which is the default), searches wrap around the end of the buffer.
given:
  b|ar
  a foo
keys:   ?foo<CR>
expect:
  bar
  a |foo

=== n wraps around too
ref:    options.txt *'wrapscan'*
quote:  Searches wrap around the end of the file.
given:  |x a1 a2
keys:   /a<CR>nn
expect: x |a1 a2

=== search with no match does not move the cursor even with wrapscan
ref:    pattern.txt *last-pattern*
quote:  If 'wrapscan' is set and the pattern was not found the error message "pattern not found" is given, and the cursor will not be moved.
given:
  one
  t|wo
keys:   /zzz<CR>
expect:
  one
  t|wo
msg:    E486: Pattern not found: zzz

=== :s sets the pattern n searches for
ref:    pattern.txt *last-pattern*
quote:  Note that two patterns are remembered: One for "normal" search commands and one for the substitute command ":s".
given:
  |foo x
  y foo
keys:   :s/foo/bar/<CR>n
expect:
  bar x
  y |foo

=== * sets the pattern used by :s//
ref:    pattern.txt *last-pattern*
quote:  Each time an empty pattern is given, the previously used pattern is used.
given:  |ab abc ab
keys:   *:s//X/g<CR>
expect: |X abc X
